winemetal: pointers of 32-bit programs in FineWine's memory window

A native arm64 Mac process has no memory below 4 GB, so in FineWine's runtime a 32-bit
program's 4 GB are a window at a base that is a multiple of 4 GB (Wine runtime patch 0043).
The 32-bit side of winemetal passes pointers as 32-bit values in 64-bit fields, which the Mac
side used as they were.

- every pointer the Mac side takes from the Windows side (parameter blocks, the structures and
  command lists they point to, the 32-bit shader compiler thunks, the cache) gets the base;
- a buffer's contents pointer going back loses the base when it is inside the window (a buffer
  placed on the program's own memory), and is otherwise left 64-bit, which the 32-bit side
  already treats as memory it cannot reach.

The base is read from ntdll.so (__wine_wow_window_base, weakly imported) and is 0 in every
process that is not 32-bit, so nothing changes for ARM64 and x64 programs.

